00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:01.910 align:start position:0%
 
ever since apple announced the switch to  their own custom apple silicon chips for  the mac lineup  intel has been under a ton of pressure  from their investors  to step up their game especially after  apple released  the m1 max which have been blowing  everyone away  so just yesterday intel announced their  weapon to fight back  and hold on to their market share and  that is of course  alder lake which is their next  generation of chips  that are actually hybrids meaning that  they'll have both big cores  and little cores which is what apple has  been doing  for years with all of their chips and  it's actually what's helped the new m1  mac  get industry leading performance and  battery life at the same time  now intel was already using this hybrid  tech with their lakefield chips  but those were heavily focused on  battery life  only having one big core with four  efficiency cores  alder lake is going to be a totally  different beast so in this video  i'm gonna explain exactly how alder lake  will work  when we can expect it to get released  how it'll compete  with apple's chips and i'm even gonna  look at some leaked alder lake  benchmarks  to see if intel can finally make a  comeback now the very first thing i want  to mention is that  when apple completes their transition to  their own ships  they will never again use intel ever  that ship has sailed and within the next  few months  apple is gonna release some new m1x  powered max and the performance is gonna  be mind-blowing  so keep in mind that intel's alder lake  will be competing  with at least the m1x not the current  and one chip  intel says that alder lake will be here  in the second half of 2021  but based on their past there's a good  chance that it's gonna get delayed just  checking out the macromers forums  there are multiple users who doubt that  intel will have them ready this year  with one user claiming that rumors are  already pointing to a 2022 release date  but enough with all of that let's dig  into how alder lake actually works  first off the most interesting detail is  that it'll use the 10  nanometer architecture not the seven  nanometer  like many people were hoping for so  that's a bit disappointing  since amd has already been making seven  nanometer chips  for an entire year already now if you're  wondering how the whole big  little chip design will work there  apparently was a leak  showing 12 different possible  configurations to the s  series of alder lake chips with the  highest one having eight big cores  eight little cores and one gpu core and  interestingly there were also some  configurations  with no little cores at all so i suspect  that those are  actually for desktop systems that don't  rely on battery power  like laptops do so they don't need any  little cores  which are meant to be efficient so the  models with the little cores are only  meant  for the laptop and mobile segment so  they can save  battery life which is one of the most  important factors in  a great laptop and that's exactly what  we saw with apple's m1  macbook pro with up to 20 hours of  battery life  which is incredible in fact apple just  published a support  article showing the difference in power  consumption and  thermal output between the m1 mac mini  and the high-end intel mac mini the  intel model basically consumes  three times more power while at idle and  while maxed out and it puts out less  than three times the amount of heat  with only a maximum of 133 btus per hour  that means a max server farm can get  three  m1 powered mac minis for the same power  and thermal price  of one intel mac mini which means that  they can save a significant amount of  money  on electricity and their cooling system  but wait  it gets even crazier the m1 mac mini is  actually eight percent faster  than the highest end intel mac mini in  terms of the cinebench r23 stress test  and then if you look at graphics  performance it's over four  times faster so this is a no-brainer  for servers showing that intel is in a  lot of trouble  in the server space as well especially  when apple releases  the m1x mac mini and this whole  efficiency difference is extremely  important for laptops  and that's why apple's macbooks can run  at full performance  while on just battery power alone while  many windows laptops  slow down drastically in order to save  battery life now  alder lake is meant to fix that problem  so let's see what intel has up their  sleeves in terms of performance  the best chip will feature 16 cores  eight big  and eight small and it looks like we  already have some leaked benchmarks from  an early engineering sample  this new chip scored 996 points in terms  of single core  and 69 31 points in multi-core which  obviously isn't great for a 16 core chip  but it seems like all of the cores were  only running at 1.38 gigahertz  for this engineering sample at least the  big core the big cores  the big cores will likely run much  faster potentially over 4.8 gigahertz  so we can expect a multi-core score of  well over 10  000 which is actually really impressive  but keep in mind  that this is the top of the line alder  lake chip but what makes it very  interesting  is that a data has been confirmed to be  the manufacturer of the ram models  4 alder lake chips with up to 64 gigs of  ram per stick  and up to a massive 8 400 megahertz  speed  which is absolutely insane  videocards.com leaked a photo of the  very first alder lake s cpu  which has a new rectangular shape using  the brand new  lga 1700 socket so it shows that apple  needs a larger socket  to fit all of that in it getting even  deeper  intel's long-term vision is to stick the  memory into the chip  package itself which is exactly what  apple  is currently doing with their custom  chips like the m1  using the unified architecture this  could help make alder lake very  competitive  especially if it supports pci express  5.0  which has been rumored for some time  this all seems to make a lot of sense  for intel  and they're even looking to scale these  hybrid chips  across their entire lineup in the future  so they seem to be going  all in on this new technology however  there's a massive hurdle that intel is  gonna have to get over  one that apple has already solved and  that's  software support while the concept of  having both big and little cores  sounds great the software has to be able  to properly utilize the cores  to make them all fire up during high  performance tasks  or for simple tasks only having the  efficiency course running  windows will have to know how to  properly handle this  as well as the app that you're running  amd actually came out and said that this  big little core design doesn't really  make sense  until software catches up because  everything from the scheduler  the memory allocator and the memory  subsystem  need to be in tune with the software so  there's a chance that intel could be  jumping the gun a little bit  unless they're secretly in talks with  windows to get  software support up and ready but in my  opinion  i think this is intel's only chance to  get back into the game  and they did mention that they'll be  implementing a next-gen  hardware scheduler alongside alder lake  so that should definitely help  with the transition now on the other  hand apple has been working with  big little arm technology for years  already  in fact the a10 fusion chip from the  iphone 7  was the first apple chip to use this  technology  and that was almost five years ago so  they have a ton of experience with it  not only that but apple has a very  unique advantage that almost nobody out  there has  they control the software the hardware  and the chip itself all at the same time  and that's why m1 max works so well if  you remember a couple of years ago when  apple dropped 32-bit app support on the  mac  or when they dropped nvidia graphics  because they refused to support apple's  metal api  all of those changes were actually part  of apple  transitioning their software years in  advance  to get ready for the m1 max  and obviously intel doesn't have that  advantage  because the only thing they control is  the chip on top of that  apple has been buying out all or most of  the yields for the best chips in the  business  from tsmc so with all of that together  it's going to be extremely hard for  intel to catch up and if you look even  further into the future  if arm chips end up outperforming x86  chips on every level  intel will be even more behind since  these new alder lake chips  are still using x86 both samsung  and qualcomm have already been making  arm-based chips for years  so i suspect many windows laptop makers  will simply go with them  instead if they want to maximize battery  life and performance  and even amd is much closer than intel  since they already create  arm-based chips like the opteron a 1100  for servers  and according to recent leaks and rumors  amd is already working on an  arm-based soc similar to apple's m1 chip  there was even amd's project sky bridge  from a few years back which intended to  pack both  x86 cores and arm cores on the same  chip being the perfect hybrid  transitionary chip  so i wonder if amd's gonna work on that  but either way  based on everything that i discussed in  this video there are a lot of hurdles  that intel is going to have to pass  through  and if they end up delaying alder lake  into 2022  they could end up way too far behind so  there you guys go  let me know what you guys think about  alder lake and the whole intel versus  apple thing  down in the comments below be sure to  check out one of those two videos right  there  click the circle above to subscribe and  we'll see you in the next video

